        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-16T19:10:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually, `bounds()` are supposed to be in device space correct? `maskBounds()` has been transformed by `localToDevice` and clipped by the scissor so using this for `Geometry::bounds()` would be incorrect if that\u0027s the case.\n\nYou mentioned in your comment in https://skia-review.googlesource.com/c/skia/+/706896 that the `stepLocalCoords` are currently incorrect. For an atlas shape, should the local coords incorporate the scale/rotate/skew that are currently included in the mask bounds? Should it take into account the clip as well? Or should `stepLocalCoords` be the untransformed, unclipped, unexpanded (due to stroke style) bounds of the original shape?",
      "parentUuid": "4ecbf301_e6fbce2f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 157,
        "startChar": 55,
        "endLine": 157,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "e640601c0645e82e8ffb672df798404366da6644",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f33c460_2eb8e977",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/geom/Geometry.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 157,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-16T23:27:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Following our offline conversation, I\u0027m now wondering if `Geometry::bounds()` should actually be the same as `Shape::bounds()` for an atlas shape (as it was earlier in this CL). An `AtlasShape` is always derived from a `Shape` after all and it makes sense to carry forward its local bounds as they should match what the client provided. OTOH `maskBounds()` is the device-space bounds of the shape restricted to the scissor rect.\n\nIs `Geometry::bounds()` used by code in a particular way that this needs to be compatible with? `AtlasShapeRenderStep` doesn\u0027t do anything with it since it just relies on `maskBounds()` and `atlasBounds()` (and it now computes `stepLocalCoords` by applying the inverse of the `localToDevice` transform to it).",
      "parentUuid": "2eb1e753_6e83fce8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 157,
        "startChar": 55,
        "endLine": 157,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "e640601c0645e82e8ffb672df798404366da6644",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c4e4d3cf_635e36df",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/geom/Geometry.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 157,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-06-22T14:46:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The only requirement of `Geometry::bounds()` is that it\u0027s the bounds *before* any transformation is applied. This is relied on in the ClipStack implementation for determining the final device space bounds.\n\nAtlasShape is a little bit unique because it\u0027s only ever created after the ClipStack processed the original Shape. The AtlasShape should then be drawn with the identity transform, which would normally be what was stored in DrawParams. But it looks like AtlasShapeRenderStep assumes this identity function and then repurposes the DrawParams\u0027 storage to use the original local-to-device matrix to reconstruct the local coords.\n\nI\u0027d like to find a way to better merge the straightforward coordinate flow of plain shapes with the two similar, but different atlas shapes (text atlases happens before clipping I believe). For now, since AtlasShape only comes after clipping, I think we are okay to move forward with this and just be aware of the inconsistency.",
      "parentUuid": "3f33c460_2eb8e977",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 157,
        "startChar": 55,
        "endLine": 157,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "e640601c0645e82e8ffb672df798404366da6644",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
